1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,500
One point six billion dollars.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:05,756
That is how much customer money was

3
00:00:05,756 --> 00:00:08,238
missing when the brokerage firm MF Global

4
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:11,148
collapsed in October twenty eleven,

5
00:00:11,148 --> 00:00:13,630
according to the trustee who took over

6
00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:14,681
the firm.

7
00:00:14,917 --> 00:00:17,797
This was not the firm's own money.

8
00:00:17,887 --> 00:00:20,127
It belonged to its customers.

9
00:00:20,277 --> 00:00:22,695
Among them were farm cooperatives

10
00:00:22,695 --> 00:00:25,356
representing up to thirty-five thousand

11
00:00:25,356 --> 00:00:26,057
farmers,

12
00:00:26,057 --> 00:00:28,717
who used the firm to protect themselves

13
00:00:28,717 --> 00:00:31,010
against swings in crop prices.

14
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:34,301
So how does customer money simply vanish

15
00:00:34,301 --> 00:00:36,680
from a regulated firm?

16
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:38,818
And did those farmers ever get it

17
00:00:38,818 --> 00:00:39,654
back?

18
00:00:39,917 --> 00:00:41,748
By the end of this video,

19
00:00:41,748 --> 00:00:44,066
you will know what a futures broker

20
00:00:44,066 --> 00:00:45,434
does with your money,

21
00:00:45,434 --> 00:00:47,265
the bet that sank MF Global,

22
00:00:47,265 --> 00:00:49,328
what happened in its last week,

23
00:00:49,328 --> 00:00:50,950
and how long it took to find

24
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:52,246
the money.

25
00:00:57,883 --> 00:01:01,123
MF Global was a futures broker.

26
00:01:01,153 --> 00:01:01,900
Farmers,

27
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:02,647
ranchers,

28
00:01:02,647 --> 00:01:05,480
traders and companies used it to buy

29
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,883
and sell futures contracts.

30
00:01:08,123 --> 00:01:10,173
A farmer could lock in a price

31
00:01:10,173 --> 00:01:12,823
for corn months before the harvest.

32
00:01:12,913 --> 00:01:15,326
A cattle rancher could protect against a

33
00:01:15,326 --> 00:01:17,023
fall in beef prices.

34
00:01:17,033 --> 00:01:19,519
That is called hedging.

35
00:01:19,783 --> 00:01:20,441
To trade,

36
00:01:20,441 --> 00:01:22,936
customers leave money with the broker as

37
00:01:22,936 --> 00:01:24,413
a deposit.

38
00:01:24,413 --> 00:01:26,025
And here is the most important rule

39
00:01:26,025 --> 00:01:27,753
in the whole business.

40
00:01:27,813 --> 00:01:30,360
That customer money must be kept separate

41
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,233
from the firm's own money.

42
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:33,887
The law says it shall not be

43
00:01:33,887 --> 00:01:35,153
commingled.

44
00:01:35,203 --> 00:01:37,442
It sits in segregated accounts,

45
00:01:37,442 --> 00:01:39,025
like a glass box the firm is

46
00:01:39,025 --> 00:01:41,887
not allowed to open for itself.

47
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:45,410
Customers trusted that rule completely.

48
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,963
One farmer later told the United States

49
00:01:47,963 --> 00:01:50,220
Senate that his broker had assured him

50
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,273
that no customer had ever lost a

51
00:01:52,273 --> 00:01:54,720
penny in segregated accounts.

52
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:57,112
He had about two hundred and fifty-three

53
00:01:57,112 --> 00:01:59,142
thousand dollars in his account when it

54
00:01:59,142 --> 00:02:00,350
was frozen.

55
00:02:00,617 --> 00:02:02,547
The firm was big.

56
00:02:02,547 --> 00:02:05,063
According to a congressional report,

57
00:02:05,063 --> 00:02:07,600
it had about thirty-six thousand futures

58
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,503
customers and more than three hundred

59
00:02:09,503 --> 00:02:12,217
securities customers when it fell.

60
00:02:12,357 --> 00:02:15,096
A senator later described its collapse as

61
00:02:15,096 --> 00:02:16,992
the eighth largest bankruptcy in the

62
00:02:16,992 --> 00:02:19,699
history of the United States.

63
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:22,870
Let me draw the glass box.

64
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:24,112
Here is the firm,

65
00:02:24,112 --> 00:02:26,740
with its own money on one side.

66
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:28,514
Here is the customer box,

67
00:02:28,514 --> 00:02:29,118
locked,

68
00:02:29,118 --> 00:02:30,550
on the other.

69
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:33,770
Farmers put their deposits into the box.

70
00:02:33,810 --> 00:02:35,735
The firm may hold the box,

71
00:02:35,735 --> 00:02:37,687
but it may not use what is

72
00:02:37,687 --> 00:02:40,240
inside to pay its own bills.

73
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:42,202
That line in the middle is the

74
00:02:42,202 --> 00:02:43,729
whole system.

75
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:45,177
In twenty ten,

76
00:02:45,177 --> 00:02:47,834
the firm got a new chief executive,

77
00:02:47,834 --> 00:02:49,896
and a new strategy.

78
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:02,757
Jon Corzine became chairman and chief

79
00:03:02,757 --> 00:03:06,189
executive of MF Global's parent company in

80
00:03:06,189 --> 00:03:07,770
March twenty ten.

81
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,689
Over the next year and a half,

82
00:03:09,689 --> 00:03:11,343
the firm built up a very large

83
00:03:11,343 --> 00:03:13,595
trading position with its own money,

84
00:03:13,595 --> 00:03:15,455
on top of handling trades for its

85
00:03:15,455 --> 00:03:16,675
customers.

86
00:03:16,917 --> 00:03:19,460
The biggest was a bet on European

87
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,047
government bonds.

88
00:03:21,047 --> 00:03:22,982
In its last earnings release,

89
00:03:22,982 --> 00:03:25,677
the firm disclosed a position of six

90
00:03:25,677 --> 00:03:27,881
point three billion dollars in short-term

91
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:29,082
bonds of Belgium,

92
00:03:29,082 --> 00:03:30,037
Italy,

93
00:03:30,037 --> 00:03:30,502
Spain,

94
00:03:30,502 --> 00:03:32,807
Portugal and Ireland.

95
00:03:32,807 --> 00:03:34,262
In twenty eleven,

96
00:03:34,262 --> 00:03:36,370
those were exactly the countries at the

97
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:38,867
center of Europe's debt crisis.

98
00:03:39,117 --> 00:03:41,446
The trades used a structure called repo

99
00:03:41,446 --> 00:03:43,017
to maturity.

100
00:03:43,147 --> 00:03:44,364
The firm bought the bonds,

101
00:03:44,364 --> 00:03:46,632
then borrowed against them until the day

102
00:03:46,632 --> 00:03:48,057
they matured.

103
00:03:48,347 --> 00:03:50,904
Under the accounting rules of the time,

104
00:03:50,904 --> 00:03:53,437
these trades were treated as sales.

105
00:03:53,457 --> 00:03:55,334
So the bonds did not appear on

106
00:03:55,334 --> 00:03:57,107
the firm's balance sheet.

107
00:03:57,247 --> 00:04:00,125
But as the industry regulator later told

108
00:04:00,125 --> 00:04:00,767
Congress,

109
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:02,760
the firm was still exposed to the

110
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:03,581
risk.

111
00:04:03,817 --> 00:04:05,182
That regulator,

112
00:04:05,182 --> 00:04:05,853
FINRA,

113
00:04:05,853 --> 00:04:08,167
decided the firm needed to hold more

114
00:04:08,167 --> 00:04:10,387
capital against those bonds.

115
00:04:10,567 --> 00:04:13,241
According to the congressional report,

116
00:04:13,241 --> 00:04:15,714
MF Global resisted throughout the summer

117
00:04:15,714 --> 00:04:17,264
of twenty eleven,

118
00:04:17,264 --> 00:04:18,838
then gave in at the end of

119
00:04:18,838 --> 00:04:19,537
August.

120
00:04:19,537 --> 00:04:21,870
The extra charges came to about two

121
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:25,037
hundred and fifty-seven million dollars.

122
00:04:25,327 --> 00:04:27,401
It had to report a capital shortfall

123
00:04:27,401 --> 00:04:28,567
for July.

124
00:04:28,867 --> 00:04:30,784
The news was in a public filing

125
00:04:30,784 --> 00:04:31,828
in September,

126
00:04:31,828 --> 00:04:33,919
but few people noticed.

127
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:37,363
Let me draw the invisible bet.

128
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:38,915
Here is the balance sheet,

129
00:04:38,915 --> 00:04:40,803
what everyone could see.

130
00:04:41,073 --> 00:04:43,099
Here are six point three billion dollars

131
00:04:43,099 --> 00:04:43,752
of bonds,

132
00:04:43,752 --> 00:04:46,603
moved off the page by the accounting.

133
00:04:46,893 --> 00:04:48,453
And here is the risk,

134
00:04:48,453 --> 00:04:50,304
still tied to the firm by a

135
00:04:50,304 --> 00:04:51,433
thin string.

136
00:04:51,623 --> 00:04:53,088
When Europe shook,

137
00:04:53,088 --> 00:04:54,884
the string pulled.

138
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:57,783
In October twenty eleven,

139
00:04:57,783 --> 00:05:00,777
that string began to pull very hard.

140
00:05:06,417 --> 00:05:07,377
On Monday,

141
00:05:07,377 --> 00:05:09,074
October twenty-fourth,

142
00:05:09,074 --> 00:05:12,027
the rating agency Moody's cut MF Global's

143
00:05:12,027 --> 00:05:15,087
credit rating to one notch above junk.

144
00:05:15,707 --> 00:05:16,618
The next day,

145
00:05:16,618 --> 00:05:19,189
the firm reported a quarterly loss of

146
00:05:19,189 --> 00:05:21,291
one hundred and ninety-one point six

147
00:05:21,291 --> 00:05:22,436
million dollars,

148
00:05:22,436 --> 00:05:24,539
and disclosed the full size of its

149
00:05:24,539 --> 00:05:26,074
European bet.

150
00:05:26,317 --> 00:05:28,837
Confidence collapsed.

151
00:05:28,977 --> 00:05:29,986
On Thursday,

152
00:05:29,986 --> 00:05:32,031
October twenty-seventh,

153
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:34,102
Moody's and Fitch both cut the firm's

154
00:05:34,102 --> 00:05:35,737
rating to junk.

155
00:05:35,847 --> 00:05:38,232
A broker that lives on trust was

156
00:05:38,232 --> 00:05:40,422
losing it by the hour.

157
00:05:40,683 --> 00:05:42,160
In those final days,

158
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,637
according to the regulator,

159
00:05:44,637 --> 00:05:47,390
the firm used customer money that was

160
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:50,243
supposed to stay in the glass box.

161
00:05:50,633 --> 00:05:53,689
The Commodity Futures Trading Commission

162
00:05:53,689 --> 00:05:56,276
later said that nearly one billion dollars

163
00:05:56,276 --> 00:05:58,862
of segregated customer funds had been

164
00:05:58,862 --> 00:06:00,873
unlawfully used.

165
00:06:01,117 --> 00:06:02,786
On October thirty-first,

166
00:06:02,786 --> 00:06:04,033
twenty eleven,

167
00:06:04,033 --> 00:06:06,759
the parent company filed for bankruptcy,

168
00:06:06,759 --> 00:06:09,083
and a trustee began to liquidate the

169
00:06:09,083 --> 00:06:10,317
brokerage.

170
00:06:10,347 --> 00:06:12,578
When the trustee opened the books,

171
00:06:12,578 --> 00:06:15,431
the customer accounts did not add up.

172
00:06:15,683 --> 00:06:18,113
Let me draw the last week.

173
00:06:18,223 --> 00:06:19,059
Monday,

174
00:06:19,059 --> 00:06:20,523
the downgrade.

175
00:06:20,683 --> 00:06:21,303
Tuesday,

176
00:06:21,303 --> 00:06:23,613
the loss and the bet revealed.

177
00:06:23,683 --> 00:06:24,650
Thursday,

178
00:06:24,650 --> 00:06:25,583
junk.

179
00:06:25,593 --> 00:06:26,345
Monday,

180
00:06:26,345 --> 00:06:27,723
bankruptcy.

181
00:06:27,813 --> 00:06:28,856
Seven days,

182
00:06:28,856 --> 00:06:30,994
from the first downgrade to a firm

183
00:06:30,994 --> 00:06:34,268
that could not find its customers' money.

184
00:06:34,517 --> 00:06:36,485
The next question was simple,

185
00:06:36,485 --> 00:06:39,327
and terrifying for the customers.

186
00:06:39,407 --> 00:06:41,475
Where did the money go?

187
00:06:47,117 --> 00:06:49,127
The answer came slowly.

188
00:06:49,127 --> 00:06:50,294
In November,

189
00:06:50,294 --> 00:06:52,438
the trustee said the shortfall could be

190
00:06:52,438 --> 00:06:54,344
as much as one point two billion

191
00:06:54,344 --> 00:06:55,897
dollars or more.

192
00:06:56,247 --> 00:06:57,676
By June twenty twelve,

193
00:06:57,676 --> 00:07:00,098
the estimate was about one point six

194
00:07:00,098 --> 00:07:01,667
billion dollars.

195
00:07:01,847 --> 00:07:04,183
About about nine hundred million dollars

196
00:07:04,183 --> 00:07:05,861
in American accounts,

197
00:07:05,861 --> 00:07:08,197
and about about seven hundred million

198
00:07:08,197 --> 00:07:10,533
dollars linked to customers trading on

199
00:07:10,533 --> 00:07:12,194
foreign exchanges.

200
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:13,741
For the customers,

201
00:07:13,741 --> 00:07:16,200
the frozen money was not abstract.

202
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:18,491
The farmer who testified to the Senate

203
00:07:18,491 --> 00:07:20,157
said he was forced to close his

204
00:07:20,157 --> 00:07:20,761
hedges,

205
00:07:20,761 --> 00:07:23,052
which cost him well over an additional

206
00:07:23,052 --> 00:07:25,110
one hundred thousand dollars.

207
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:26,780
His words were blunt.

208
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:29,768
Commingling money is stealing money.

209
00:07:30,017 --> 00:07:31,281
At the same hearing,

210
00:07:31,281 --> 00:07:33,852
a farm cooperative leader asked Congress

211
00:07:33,852 --> 00:07:36,423
to ensure the sanctity of segregated

212
00:07:36,423 --> 00:07:37,237
funds.

213
00:07:37,437 --> 00:07:39,487
And a senator called the collapse a

214
00:07:39,487 --> 00:07:41,536
violation of the most sacred rule of

215
00:07:41,536 --> 00:07:43,440
the futures industry.

216
00:07:43,683 --> 00:07:45,773
Let me draw the hole.

217
00:07:45,893 --> 00:07:47,436
Here is the glass box,

218
00:07:47,436 --> 00:07:49,343
as it should have been.

219
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:51,583
Here is what the trustee found.

220
00:07:51,583 --> 00:07:53,643
A gap of about one point six

221
00:07:53,643 --> 00:07:55,273
billion dollars.

222
00:07:55,413 --> 00:07:56,297
On one side,

223
00:07:56,297 --> 00:07:58,463
the American accounts.

224
00:07:58,463 --> 00:07:59,503
On the other,

225
00:07:59,503 --> 00:08:02,013
the money tied to trades abroad.

226
00:08:02,123 --> 00:08:04,573
And thousands of customers waiting

227
00:08:04,573 --> 00:08:05,717
outside.

228
00:08:05,983 --> 00:08:08,092
Then came the long work of getting

229
00:08:08,092 --> 00:08:08,856
it back,

230
00:08:08,856 --> 00:08:11,229
and of deciding who would be held

231
00:08:11,229 --> 00:08:12,884
responsible.

232
00:08:18,550 --> 00:08:19,600
Piece by piece,

233
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:23,160
the trustee paid customers back in stages.

234
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:24,948
By November twenty thirteen,

235
00:08:24,948 --> 00:08:26,957
the securities customers had their

236
00:08:26,957 --> 00:08:28,360
property back.

237
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:30,136
By April twenty fourteen,

238
00:08:30,136 --> 00:08:32,930
he announced a final distribution.

239
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:33,939
In total,

240
00:08:33,939 --> 00:08:36,419
about six point seven billion dollars had

241
00:08:36,419 --> 00:08:38,674
gone back to more than twenty-six thousand

242
00:08:38,674 --> 00:08:39,950
customers.

243
00:08:40,230 --> 00:08:40,466
In his words,

244
00:08:40,466 --> 00:08:41,410
they were made one hundred percent whole.

245
00:08:41,410 --> 00:08:42,377
The biggest group,

246
00:08:42,377 --> 00:08:44,549
about twenty-four thousand American

247
00:08:44,549 --> 00:08:45,911
futures customers,

248
00:08:45,911 --> 00:08:47,885
received about five point four billion

249
00:08:47,885 --> 00:08:48,700
dollars.

250
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:51,015
It had taken about two and a

251
00:08:51,015 --> 00:08:52,129
half years.

252
00:08:52,383 --> 00:08:53,829
In twenty thirteen,

253
00:08:53,829 --> 00:08:57,014
a federal court order required MF Global's

254
00:08:57,014 --> 00:08:59,220
brokerage to pay one point two one

255
00:08:59,220 --> 00:09:01,915
two billion dollars in restitution to

256
00:09:01,915 --> 00:09:02,871
customers,

257
00:09:02,871 --> 00:09:06,032
plus a one hundred million dollar penalty,

258
00:09:06,032 --> 00:09:08,728
to be paid only after the customers

259
00:09:08,728 --> 00:09:10,063
had been paid.

260
00:09:10,413 --> 00:09:13,378
The regulator later said restitution had

261
00:09:13,378 --> 00:09:16,487
been paid to satisfy all customer claims.

262
00:09:16,750 --> 00:09:19,372
And in January twenty seventeen,

263
00:09:19,372 --> 00:09:22,259
Jon Corzine settled the regulator's civil

264
00:09:22,259 --> 00:09:23,100
case.

265
00:09:23,170 --> 00:09:24,997
He agreed to pay a five million

266
00:09:24,997 --> 00:09:27,230
dollars civil penalty and never to

267
00:09:27,230 --> 00:09:29,860
register with the commission again.

268
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:32,508
He settled without admitting or denying

269
00:09:32,508 --> 00:09:33,355
the charges,

270
00:09:33,355 --> 00:09:36,126
and he was not criminally charged.

271
00:09:36,383 --> 00:09:39,133
The rules changed too.

272
00:09:39,353 --> 00:09:40,960
In late twenty thirteen,

273
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:43,754
the regulator approved new customer

274
00:09:43,754 --> 00:09:45,053
protections.

275
00:09:45,243 --> 00:09:47,708
Brokers must now report their segregated

276
00:09:47,708 --> 00:09:49,254
accounts every day,

277
00:09:49,254 --> 00:09:51,718
and large withdrawals of customer money

278
00:09:51,718 --> 00:09:54,783
need approval from senior management.

279
00:09:55,063 --> 00:09:57,275
The chairman of the commission called

280
00:09:57,275 --> 00:09:59,708
segregation the core foundation of the

281
00:09:59,708 --> 00:10:00,751
markets.

282
00:10:01,017 --> 00:10:03,087
Let me draw the repair.

283
00:10:03,087 --> 00:10:05,527
Here is the glass box again.

284
00:10:05,527 --> 00:10:06,877
Now add a camera,

285
00:10:06,877 --> 00:10:08,837
watching it every day.

286
00:10:08,837 --> 00:10:10,324
Now add a second key,

287
00:10:10,324 --> 00:10:12,627
held by a senior manager.

288
00:10:12,627 --> 00:10:14,857
And a phone line to the regulator.

289
00:10:14,887 --> 00:10:16,745
The box was always supposed to be

290
00:10:16,745 --> 00:10:17,647
locked.

291
00:10:17,647 --> 00:10:20,348
Now someone is watching the lock.

292
00:10:20,583 --> 00:10:23,210
So how did one point six billion

293
00:10:23,210 --> 00:10:25,123
dollars vanish?

